#
# Local Docker overrides.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (which is gitignored) and uncomment what you need.
# `docker compose` reads `.env` from the project root automatically, so no
# tracked file has to be modified -- and nothing shows up in `git status`.
#
# Every value below defaults to what `docker-compose.yaml` used to hardcode,
# so leaving `.env` absent keeps the out-of-the-box behaviour unchanged.
#

#
# Host HTTPS port. Default 443.
#
# Changing this is fully supported: every site is then reachable at
# `https://central.wordcamp.test:8443/` and the like, with no database changes
# and nothing to re-run. `.docker/wp-config.php` keeps the port out of every
# hostname comparison, and the `0-local-https-port` mu-plugin puts it back onto
# generated URLs.
#
# Note that plain HTTP stops redirecting correctly when this isn't 443 -- nginx
# can't know which host port Docker mapped 443 to -- so use `https://` directly.
# For the same reason, changing WORDCAMP_HTTP_PORT isn't worth doing.
#
#WORDCAMP_HTTPS_PORT=8443
#WORDCAMP_HTTP_PORT=80

#
# Host interface the containers bind to. Unset by default, which binds every
# interface over both IPv4 and IPv6 -- including your LAN address.
#
# Set it to restrict the stack to one address. Site URLs are unchanged (no port
# suffix, no hosts-file edit), the dev site stops being reachable from the rest
# of the network, and another stack can then bind 443 on a *different* address.
#
# This does not free up `0.0.0.0:443` -- something that wants every interface
# will still collide. Change WORDCAMP_HTTPS_PORT instead for that.
#
#WORDCAMP_BIND_IP=127.0.0.1

#
# MailCatcher and MariaDB host ports. Safe to change freely.
#
#WORDCAMP_MAILCATCHER_PORT=1080
#WORDCAMP_DB_PORT=3307
